  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Words and music by Hughie Cannon, first published 1902 by Howley, Haviland and Dresser, New York; US copyright long expired. Cannon died in 1912, so the composition is also clear on the author-died-pre-1930 test.",
    "sourceEdition": "Cannon, Hughie, 'Bill Bailey, Won't You Please Come Home?', Howley, Haviland & Dresser, New York, 1902 (first edition, 2/4 in G); read against the standard bar-paced traditional-jazz chorus chart, which writes the same chorus as 32 bars of 4/4",
    "notes": "Chorus only; melody and changes, no lyrics. The 1902 print is 2/4, so this is encoded as 4/4 at half the written tempo -- one bar here is two printed bars and the 32-bar chorus becomes 16. That compression is why some sub-bar changes in the standard chart cannot be carried: the passing diminished on 'whole day', the D7 inside 'fine tooth comb', the diminished on 'blame, well', and the Dm7-Gm7 turnarounds all collapse into their bar's primary chord. The song's minstrel-dialect verse text is not carried."
  },
  "key": "G",
  "mode": "major",
  "meter": "4/4",
  "tempoBPM": 116,
  "tempoRange": [88, 152],
  "feel": "swing",
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "16-bar chorus split into two 8-bar heads so the D7 answer and the C-major turn each land as their own Part. Three chromatics are the tune, not ornament, and a snap-to-chord treatment must not iron them out: the A# on 'Bill Bai-ley' and again on 'rain-y eve' (deg 2, acc 1); the F natural on 'fine tooth comb' over G7 (deg 7, acc -1); the G# on 'Bai-ley, won't you' (deg 1, acc 1). The dotted-eighth/sixteenth on 'home, Bill' and 'cook-ing, dar-' is the figure that makes the tune recognisable -- do not flatten it to even eighths.",
